---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import {i18nSidebar} from '../../../data/i18n-sidebar';
import {Code} from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Locales & Resolution" sidebar={i18nSidebar}
            description="The Locale type, the fallback chain, and how a message is found">

    <h1>Locales &amp; Resolution</h1>

    <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
        Two axes: the locale chain from most to least specific, and catalog precedence within each locale.
    </p>

    <h2>Locale</h2>

    <p>A locale is a language plus an optional region, in BCP-47 shape. Normalization happens at
        construction, so casing and separator never matter.</p>

    <Code code={`Locale("de")             // de
Locale("de", "CH")       // de-CH
Locale.parse("de_ch")    // de-CH  — underscore and casing normalized
Locale.parse("DE")       // de

Locale.parse("de-CH").base   // de     — the language without the region
Locale("de").base            // de     — a base locale returns itself
Locale.parse("de-CH").tag    // "de-CH"`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p><strong>Always parse untrusted tags.</strong> A language stored on a user record is free-form text;
        <code>Locale.parse</code> is what makes a stored <code>"DE"</code> or <code>"de_CH"</code> resolve
        like the <code>de-CH</code> it means instead of missing every catalog and falling to English.</p>

    <h2>The fallback chain</h2>

    <p>Resolution walks four positions, most specific first, de-duplicated:</p>

    <Code code={`locale -> locale.base -> fallback -> fallback.base`} lang="text" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>With <code>locale = de-CH</code> and <code>fallback = en</code> that is
        <code>de-CH</code>, <code>de</code>, <code>en</code>. So a Swiss catalog only needs to carry what
        actually differs from German &mdash; everything else falls through.</p>

    <h2>Two axes, and the order matters</h2>

    <p>The locale chain is the <strong>outer</strong> loop; catalog precedence is the <strong>inner</strong>
        one. The first hit wins.</p>

    <Code code={`for (locale in chain) {          // de-CH, de, en
    for (catalog in catalogs) {   // app, then framework
        // first hit wins
    }
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>So a more specific locale always beats a less specific one, and within the same locale the app beats
        the framework. An app's German string beats the framework's German string; the framework's German
        string beats the app's English one.</p>

    <Aside>Locale-outer is the right default for message catalogs, where a key is a fragment and getting the
        language right matters more than which module supplied it. It is worth knowing this is a decision
        rather than an inevitability &mdash; funktor's email templates deliberately invert it, because a
        branded mail in the wrong language beats an unbranded one in the right language.
    </Aside>

    <h2>Switching language</h2>

    <p><code>I18n</code> is immutable. Changing language produces a new instance carrying the same catalogs:</p>

    <Code code={`val german = i18n.withLocale(Locale("de"))`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark"/>

    <p>That is what makes the reactive integration straightforward &mdash; a stream emits a new snapshot and
        anything subscribed re-renders. Nothing mutates under a component that is mid-render.</p>

    <h2>Two surfaces</h2>

    <p><code>i18n.translate</code> is the <strong>open</strong> surface: module-generated namespaces hang off
        it as extension properties, which is how a module contributes translations without the core knowing
        about it.</p>

    <p><code>i18n.format</code> is the <strong>closed</strong> surface for dates, numbers and currency. It is
        currently a seam that exposes the locale and little else &mdash; CLDR-backed formatting is not
        implemented yet. Route formatting through it anyway, so the implementation can land later without
        moving call sites.</p>

    <h2>Known limits</h2>

    <p>Script and variant subtags are not modelled. The second subtag is always treated as a region and
        upper-cased, so <code>zh-Hant</code> parses as <code>zh-HANT</code> and will not match a script-aware
        catalog. Do not rely on those tags until script support lands.</p>

    <div class="not-prose mt-8">
        <a href="/ultra/i18n/kraft"
           class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
            Kraft integration &rarr;
        </a>
    </div>

</DocsLayout>
